(the end of the eighteenth century. American Upland cotton, by reason of) Tj T*
(its comparatively short staple, and the unevenness of the fibers, as well) Tj T*
(as the difficulty of detaching it from the seed, was decidedly inferior) Tj T*
(to some other accessible species. The Southern planters who grew it,) Tj T*
(moreover, found it next to impossible to gin it properly, the primitive) Tj T*
(roller gin of the time being unsuited to the task, and the work of) Tj T*
(pulling off the fibers by hand being both tedious and expensive. In 1792,) Tj T*
(the amount exported from the United States was equivalent to only 275) Tj T*
(bales.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: _Eli Whitney, the schoolmaster inventor of the cotton) Tj T*
(gin_]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The next year, 1793, is the most important in the history of cotton) Tj T*
(growing in the United States. In the autumn of 1792, Eli Whitney, a young) Tj T*
(Massachusetts man who had just been graduated from Yale College, sailed) Tj T*
(from New York to South Carolina where he intended to teach school. On) Tj T*
(shipboard he met the widow of Nathaniel Greene, the Revolutionary) Tj T*
(general. Mrs. Greene invited the youth to begin his residence in the) Tj T*
(South on her plantation at Mulberry Grove, Georgia. Here one evening,) Tj T*
(some officers, late of General Greene's command, were discussing the) Tj T*
(great wealth which might come to the South were there a suitable machine) Tj T*
(for removing stubborn Upland fiber from its green seed. The story goes) Tj T*
(that while the discussion was at its height, Mrs. Greene said:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Gentlemen, apply to my young friend, Mr. Whitney. He can make) Tj T*
(anything.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Whitney commenced work on the problem. A room was set aside as his) Tj T*
(workshop, and it was not long before he had produced the beginnings of) Tj T*
(the gin. He fixed wire teeth in a board, and found that by pulling the) Tj T*
(fibers through with his fingers he could leave the tenacious seed behind.) Tj T*
(He carried this basic idea further by putting the teeth on a cylinder and) Tj T*
(by providing a rotating brush to clean the fiber from the teeth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The changes which followed immediately upon the introduction of the) Tj T*
(cotton gin were tremendous in scope and almost innumerable. There was a) Tj T*
(time, before cotton became a staple, when the South led New England in) Tj T*
(manufacturing. That time passed almost immediately. Iron works and coal) Tj T*
(mines were abandoned, and men turned their energies from the culture of) Tj T*
(corn, rice, and indigo largely to the raising of the cotton.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
