(By this table it will be seen that the Civil War and the freeing of the) Tj T*
(slaves held up production only temporarily. In 1914, the banner year, the) Tj T*
(crop reached the tremendous total of 16,134,930 bales of five hundred) Tj T*
(pounds each.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some little spinning had been done in the seventeenth century, but in) Tj T*
(1787-88 the first permanent factory, built of brick, and located in) Tj T*
(Beverly, Massachusetts, on the Bass river, was put into operation by a) Tj T*
(group headed by John Cabot and Joshua Fisher. This factory failed to) Tj T*
(justify itself economically, chiefly because of the crudeness of its) Tj T*
(machinery. But Samuel Slater, newly come from England with models of the) Tj T*
(Arkwright machinery in his brain, set up a factory in Pawtucket in 1790.) Tj T*
(From that time forth the growth was steady and sure, if not always) Tj T*
(extremely rapid.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
