(For Long Staples) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A few years ago the terms short-staple and Upland were practically) Tj T*
(interchangeable, but the great demand for long staple, chiefly from the) Tj T*
(manufacturers of thread and of pneumatic tire fabrics has led to a) Tj T*
(successful attempt to grow the longer fibers in the Upland districts, so) Tj T*
(that now more than a million bales annually are being produced in the) Tj T*
(Upland districts of cotton with a staple length of 1-1/8 inches and more.) Tj T*
(The world's total production of long staple is in the neighborhood of) Tj T*
(2,250,000 bales. Egypt is the chief producer outside the United States,) Tj T*
(her product being approximately 1,000,000 bales of 500 pounds every year.) Tj T*
(Although the product is small, the best Sea Island produced in the United) Tj T*
(States grows upon the small islands off the coast of South Carolina. The) Tj T*
(long-staple Upland is grown chiefly in the Mississippi delta, where the) Tj T*
(product is called "Peeler," "benders," etc., though the percentage of) Tj T*
(long-staple produced elsewhere is steadily increasing. The success of) Tj T*
(certain Arizona growers in producing long-staple from Egyptian seed is) Tj T*
(being watched with great interest. More than 3,000 bales came from this) Tj T*
(source in 1916, the fiber averaging 1-1/2 inches in length. There has) Tj T*
(recently been developed there, the new and important Pima variety, which) Tj T*
(is superior to the native Egyptian cotton, being both longer and whiter,) Tj T*
(and the growers are now planting Pima almost exclusively.) Tj T*
