(If, however, the manufacturer sells "futures" for the succeeding months) Tj T*
(to the extent of the cotton which he would require each month to) Tj T*
(manufacture the goods, he can run his machinery as usual and have a) Tj T*
(perfectly free mind, as he has safeguarded himself against any loss due) Tj T*
(to a falling value of the raw material. Suppose, for instance, the cotton) Tj T*
(market fell off, say one cent a pound each month, with a corresponding) Tj T*
(fall in the value of the woven goods. In such an event, the manufacturer) Tj T*
(could, as each month arrived, buy a contract for an amount corresponding) Tj T*
(to what he had sold, and at a proportionately less price, thus making a) Tj T*
(profit on the "futures" which he had sold to an extent which would) Tj T*
(correspond, approximately, to the smaller value which his manufactured) Tj T*
(goods would then have in the market. Thus the profit on the one side) Tj T*
(would take care of the loss on the other. If the market rose instead of) Tj T*
(falling, he would make a loss in replacing his futures contract, but his) Tj T*
(goods would then command a higher value, and again no loss would be) Tj T*
(experienced.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This method of hedging is the regular and standard practice of the) Tj T*
(English cotton mills, and, of course, of many of our domestic mills, but) Tj T*
(there are some manufacturers who, through their unfamiliarity with the) Tj T*
(operations of the futures market, are quite unaware of the protection) Tj T*
(which they thus have at hand.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Responsiveness of) Tj T*
(the Great Exchanges) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The great exchanges, and the New York Exchange in particular, are thus) Tj T*
(used by cotton merchants and manufacturers in every part of the world to) Tj T*
(protect themselves in their buying and selling operations. The value of) Tj T*
(middling cotton in New York is kept upon par with the value of the same) Tj T*
(cotton in any growing or manufacturing point, such factors as freight,) Tj T*
(insurance, brokerage, etc., being allowed for in the quoted price.) Tj T*
(Quotations on the Liverpool Exchange are thus higher than quotations in) Tj T*
(New York by the difference between the amount it costs to deliver cotton) Tj T*
(in Liverpool and to deliver it in New York. Thus the merchant and) Tj T*
(manufacturer is able to buy and sell hedge contracts on the New York) Tj T*
(Exchange, knowing that operations at the New York price in New York are) Tj T*
(on a parity with operations at the Liverpool price in Liverpool, or at) Tj T*
(the Havre price in Havre. Thus the hedge contract which a Southern) Tj T*
(merchant sells in Atlanta, through his broker on the New York Exchange,) Tj T*
(may be bought by a spinner in Tokyo or Manchester, anxious to insure his) Tj T*
(supply of cotton at a price which would make his contracts profitable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In normal times the selling of merchants and the buying of manufacturing) Tj T*
(engaged in actual and bona fide hedging transaction has been estimated by) Tj T*
(competent authorities to make up fully seventy-five per cent. of the) Tj T*
(trading done on the New York Exchange. The remaining twenty-five per) Tj T*
