(Early in the spring, the farm hands begin the work of getting the seed) Tj T*
(beds ready. Upland fields have to be terraced, ditched, and drained by an) Tj T*
(elaborate process before the work is well begun. Plowing and sub-soiling) Tj T*
(are the least of the planter's worries. He must often chop last year's) Tj T*
(stalks with a disc harrow or with a stalk cutter. The spike tooth or the) Tj T*
(disc harrow must work again after the plowing is finished. It is) Tj T*
(customary to plant cotton in a slightly raised bed, in order that) Tj T*
(thinning may be more easily done, and that the soil may be more quickly) Tj T*
(warmed. Much planting is still done by hand, one man dropping the seeds) Tj T*
(in the long straight furrow and another following close behind him with a) Tj T*
(hoe, covering them up; but of late years the one-horse planter and the) Tj T*
(two-horse combined lister and planter have come into vogue, and, now that) Tj T*
(the tractor is both cheap and serviceable, it is possible to plant two or) Tj T*
(more rows at a time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Long Season of) Tj T*
(Intensive Cultivation) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When the tiny seedlings first appear above the fragrant mellow soil, the) Tj T*
(planter's work is well begun, but it is only begun, for then comes the) Tj T*
(season of cultivating and thinning out. As soon as there are two or three) Tj T*
(inches of growth, the first cultivation takes place. How many times the) Tj T*
(field is cultivated depends on the planter, the nature of the soil, the) Tj T*
(availability of labor and other factors. But the general rule is, the) Tj T*
(more cultivations, the more cotton. The first cultivation scrapes away) Tj T*
(the soil from the plants, leaving them on a small ridge, where the) Tj T*
(thinning-out process can easily be done with a hoe. The stalks are left) Tj T*
(from fifteen to twenty inches apart in the hill, the rows being usually) Tj T*
(about three and a half feet apart. The next cultivation, usually with a) Tj T*
(sweep, pushes the soil back against the plants. Then begins the farmer's) Tj T*
(fight against the weeds, each of which seems sturdier and harder to) Tj T*
(eradicate than its predecessor. Usually cultivation must take place about) Tj T*
(every three weeks.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In June, on the average, the bell-shaped blossoms appear. On the first) Tj T*
(day they are cream colored or white; on the second day, they change to a) Tj T*
(beautiful wild-rose pink, deepening toward evening to a deeper magenta or) Tj T*
(carnation. On the third day they fade completely, and the development of) Tj T*
(the boll begins.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
