(In the revolving flat card, which dominates the field today, there are,) Tj T*
(as a rule, three principal cylinders. The lap passes first under the) Tj T*
(smallest of the three, called the taker-in, which is covered with very) Tj T*
(fine saw-teeth all in one long strip of steel, wound and fixed spirally) Tj T*
(in the surface of the cylinder. The taker-in receives the cotton from a) Tj T*
(feed-roller \(C\) that turns above a smooth iron plate \(D\) called the feed) Tj T*
(plate. The saw-teeth comb the fibers which are imbedded, so to speak, in) Tj T*
(the lap, and deliver the loose ones to the second cylinder, which is the) Tj T*
(largest of the group. This main cylinder is covered with wire teeth all) Tj T*
(bent at exactly the same angle. The cotton clings to them, and is carried) Tj T*
(around to the top of the cylinder, where it is engaged by teeth on the) Tj T*
(revolving-flat card which are bent in the opposite direction. This) Tj T*
("card-clothing" arranged in strip, crosswise on a travelling lattice,) Tj T*
(moves in the same direction as the cylinder but moves very slowly, and so) Tj T*
(the fibers are carded between the two sets of wire points, the short and) Tj T*
(immature fibers remaining on the card wires of the lattice and the) Tj T*
(perfect and now almost entirely parallel ones being carried over from the) Tj T*
(main cylinder to the doffer cylinder, the third of the trio. From this) Tj T*
(they are removed by an oscillating comb \(F\), coming off in a light,) Tj T*
