(In the bleaching of cotton, there is a series of operations which have) Tj T*
(for their object the elimination of the waxy, fatty matters embodied in) Tj T*
(the fiber, as well as any dirt which it may have acquired. Then, there is) Tj T*
(the actual whitening and the bleaching of the cloth which destroys any) Tj T*
(coloring matter which it may contain and finally there are treatments) Tj T*
(designed to neutralize the effect of the chemicals used in the bleaching.) Tj T*
(Thus, the sequence of treatments might be: first, boiling in plain water,) Tj T*
(which removes certain soluble substances; next, an extended boiling in a) Tj T*
(strong alkaline solution, which saponifies the waxy, fatty matters in the) Tj T*
(fiber, and thus removes them from the cloth or yarn. Third, a steeping in) Tj T*
(a bleaching solution--a solution of chloride of lime being largely) Tj T*
