() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Native Stock) Tj T*
(in Southern Mills) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Conditions in the South were somewhat different. From the beginning, the) Tj T*
(employes here have been almost entirely of native stock. They came from a) Tj T*
(class which previously had little opportunity for any employment of a) Tj T*
(regular character outside of farming. When the mills were built these) Tj T*
(folks were given, for the first time, an opportunity for continuous) Tj T*
(employment. Whole families entered the mills, fathers, mothers and) Tj T*
(children serving in different or in the same departments. The South at) Tj T*
(first specialized on ducks, twills, denims, and such coarse work. Now,) Tj T*
(however, there is a growing tendency to diversify the product. The reason) Tj T*
(is found in the increasing capability of the workers, many of whom have) Tj T*
(by now spent many years of their lives in the mills, and whose fathers) Tj T*
(before them were operatives. Unless present conditions change and the) Tj T*
(South becomes the mecca of immigrants--a development probably less likely) Tj T*
(now than in the years before the war--there seems to be a strong) Tj T*
(possibility that a class of operatives, rivalling eventually in skill) Tj T*
(those of the English mill towns, will be developed. The stock is the) Tj T*
(same, and the latent capabilities are all there. The determining factors) Tj T*
(will probably be the economic changes of the next few years.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A remaining factor in the organization of the mill is the size of the) Tj T*
(individual plant, the number of spindles and looms it contains, the) Tj T*
(number of workers employed, etc. It is in just this particular that some) Tj T*
(of the most characteristic developments of the American industry are) Tj T*
(found. About the time of the Civil War, the average New England mill had) Tj T*
(less than ten thousand spindles. Today the average is probably between) Tj T*
(fifty and one hundred thousand, and perhaps nearer the latter figure than) Tj T*
(the former. Some of the mills have nearly, if not quite, a full million) Tj T*
(spindles in several buildings. The average in the South is much less than) Tj T*
(the New England average. The industry in the older section is definitely) Tj T*
(localized, even to the extent of having whole towns devoted almost) Tj T*
(exclusively to the manufacture of single grades of cloth. In the South) Tj T*
(the mills are more widely scattered, advantage having been taken of labor) Tj T*
(supply, water power, and other conditions. Local pride has sometimes) Tj T*
(caused the establishment of mills in regions economically unfitted for) Tj T*
(them. Such mills do not long survive. The advantage of large scale) Tj T*
(production has thus been seized chiefly by the New England mills, but the) Tj T*
(generally lower wages of the South have tended to equalize the) Tj T*
(situation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
